{"abstract":"Effects of embodying virtual avatars are routinely validated experimentally by comparing synchronous and asynchronous movements between virtual and real bodies. This experimental paradigm, however, lacks justification, validation, and standardization. Asynchrony is implemented in numerous ways, such as through delayed, dislocated, or prerecorded movements, and these may impact embodiment and user experience distinctively. An online study (N = 202) revealed that variations of asynchrony cause disparate responses to embodiment and user experience, with prerecorded movements distorting embodiment the most. A think-aloud study (N = 16) revealed that asynchronous conditions lead to peculiar and oftentimes negative experiences. Furthermore, asynchronous conditions in some cases maintain, rather than break the body ownership illusion, as participants imitate the virtual body. Our results show that asynchrony in experiments on embodiment entails profound validity issues and should therefore be used with caution.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Iarygina, Olga","Hornbæk, Kasper","Mottelson, Aske"],"cuid":["S02-082.69-813959"],"event":{"end":"2025-05-01","place":"Yokohama, jp","start":"2025-04-26","title":"2025 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems, CHI 2025","year":"2025"},"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Body ownership illusions","Confounds","Embodiment","Experiments","Validity","Virtual reality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"does random movements mean random results why asynchrony in experiments on body ownership does not work as intended","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3706598.3713506","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-082.69-813959","identifier":[{"id":"105005721021","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5076f290-7230-4304-a933-247cf1f20c86","ku-b03f4b2b-07ca-4f91-a556-63dedf574db1"],"keyword":["body ownership illusions","confounds","embodiment","experiments","validity","virtual reality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-783EF4BA6B0F4F6723A9560E33EB844C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3706598.3713506","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/453747361/Does_Random_Movements.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centred Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Olga","key":"iaryginapau","last":"Iarygina","locid":"977ec015-b047-44f1-8aa9-bda7d606c977","name":"Iarygina, Olga","orcid":"0000-0002-2419-9638","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Kasper","key":"hornbækpau","last":"Hornbæk","locid":"148d1e88-e306-46e1-9218-f420470b1ce1","name":"Hornbæk, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0001-8742-1198","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"410df04c-bc1d-4cd3-9726-c35469ddab8c","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3706598.3713506","eisbn":["9798400713941"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI 2025 - Proceedings of the 2025 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105005721021"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5076f290-7230-4304-a933-247cf1f20c86","ku-b03f4b2b-07ca-4f91-a556-63dedf574db1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Does Random Movements mean Random Results? Why Asynchrony in Experiments on Body Ownership does not Work as Intended","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-082.69-813959","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Demand characteristics refer to cues that can inform participants in experiments about the hypothesis and influence their behavior. They lead researchers to erroneously infer non-existing effects, undermining the experimental integrity of empirical studies. Despite a widespread acknowledgment of their confounding influence in experimental psychology, experiments involving humans and computers to a lesser extent consider effects of demand characteristics, as computerized protocols are thought to be immune to some experimenter biases. Furthermore, demand characteristics are considered to mainly effect subjective measures. As a result, demand characteristics often remain uncontrolled in studies involving computers, and in particular for objective measures such as performance. In this paper, we present two experiments that underline the importance of demand characteristics in human–computer interaction experiments. In a text-entry study, we made participants believe they were evaluating a research-based keyboard. This belief led to increased performance and self-reported user experience. In a second study, we conducted a thought experiment on the illusion of body ownership in virtual reality, where the experimental design indicated the study hypothesis. We found hypothesis-compliant responses from participants, even when they did not experience the illusion. We conclude that demand characteristics pose a significant challenge to the interpretation and validity of human–computer experiments, even when they are fully automated. We discuss the implications and offer guidelines to mitigate effects of demand characteristics.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Iarygina, Olga","Hornbæk, Kasper","Mottelson, Aske"],"cuid":["S02-095.74-785373"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Bias","Confounds","Demand characteristics","Experiments","Human Computer Interaction","Validity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"demand characteristics in human computer experiments","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhcs.2024.103379","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-095.74-785373","identifier":[{"id":"85205678895","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-fddae596-5117-4ea3-b737-b1382bad78ca","ku-744d9c94-711a-4a38-a2a6-068620251e0b"],"keyword":["Bias","Confounds","Demand characteristics","Experiments","Human–computer interaction","Validity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-1A1D63F4DB8BDABA60C1C53A8353117E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1071581924001629/pdfft?md5=4694005704dd14bd4ab5dca531896862&pid=1-s2.0-S1071581924001629-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/411263560/Demand_characteristics_in_human_computer_experiments.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhcs.2024.103379","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centred Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Olga","key":"iaryginapau","last":"Iarygina","locid":"977ec015-b047-44f1-8aa9-bda7d606c977","name":"Iarygina, Olga","orcid":"0000-0002-2419-9638","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Kasper","key":"hornbækpau","last":"Hornbæk","locid":"148d1e88-e306-46e1-9218-f420470b1ce1","name":"Hornbæk, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0001-8742-1198","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"410df04c-bc1d-4cd3-9726-c35469ddab8c","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ijhcs.2024.103379","issn":["10715819","10959300"],"issn_org":["10715819"],"journal":"International Journal of Human-Computer Studies","paperid":"103379","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Human-Computer Studies","title_org":"International Journal of Human Computer Studies","volume":"193"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85205678895"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-fddae596-5117-4ea3-b737-b1382bad78ca","ku-744d9c94-711a-4a38-a2a6-068620251e0b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Demand characteristics in human–computer experiments","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.74-785373","year":2025,"yearsub":2025}
{"abstract":"Text entry is a notably standardized research field in human-computer interaction, with established benchmarks and methodologies enabling rigorous comparisons across studies. Nevertheless, meta research in text-entry is scarce. This meta-analysis summarizes findings and effects from text entry experiments published from 1990 to 2024. Our records show that most text-entry experiments feature a baseline and an experimental UI, and that they mostly show that the experimental UI is superior (g = 1.68), with regards to text entry speed. We found that earlier text entry research focused mostly on the development of novel techniques, whereas recent text entry research, to a larger extent, adapts existing input methods to new devices, such as smartwatches or virtual reality headsets. We also find that text entry research often lacks statistical power (M = 0.66, SD = 0.37), relies on small sample sizes (Mdn = 12), and is mostly conducted with within-subjects designs (84%). Subjective evaluations of text entry systems beyond objective performance are rare. Our analysis found evidence for systematic publication bias in text entry research, as indicated by funnel plot asymmetry and a significant weight-function model adjustment. This underlines the research field’s competitive culture of publishing research only if entry speed is beaten in comparison to some baseline.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Iarygina, Olga","Mottelson, Aske"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818248"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Keyboards","Meta analysis","Text entry"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a systematic review and meta analysis of text entry studies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105005728890","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-818248","identifier":[{"id":"105005728890","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-95001c8e-b9e9-44c4-b654-ca8bf98db9e2"],"keyword":["Keyboards","Meta-analysis","Text entry"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-2FBB6BCF6EEF3C82BCD501A7D853A5FD"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Olga","key":"iaryginapau","last":"Iarygina","locid":"977ec015-b047-44f1-8aa9-bda7d606c977","name":"Iarygina, Olga","orcid":"0000-0002-2419-9638","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"410df04c-bc1d-4cd3-9726-c35469ddab8c","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Naomi","key":"yamashitapedh","last":"Yamashita","name":"Yamashita, Naomi","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Vanessa","key":"everspedh","last":"Evers","name":"Evers, Vanessa","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Koji","key":"yatanipedh","last":"Yatani","name":"Yatani, Koji","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Xianghua (Sharon)","key":"dingpedh","last":"Ding","name":"Ding, Xianghua (Sharon)","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3706599.3720117","eisbn":["9798400713958"],"pages":"1-14","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI EA '25: Proceedings of the Extended Abstracts of the CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105005728890"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-95001c8e-b9e9-44c4-b654-ca8bf98db9e2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Text Entry Studies","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818248","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"In a seminal paper, Banakou et al. (2013) showed that illusory ownership of a child’s body experienced through virtual reality (VR) changes perceptions and attitudes. The illusion of having the body of a 4-year-old child led participants to overestimate object sizes and react faster when associating the self with child-like attributes in an Implicit Association Test. The paper has become a model for conducting experimental embodiment research, and has impacted the study of how bodies shape minds considerably. Despite its massive impact, the study is underpowered, does not control for demand characteristics, and uses unvalidated subjective scales. The evidence of behavioral effects caused by the embodiment is therefore uncertain. To address this, we will perform a large-scale partial replication, updated to refined methodological practices with regard to objective and subjective measures, controlling for demand characteristics, and increasing the statistical power to 95%.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Iarygina, Olga","Hornbæk, Kasper","Mottelson, Aske"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776425"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Embodiment","Implicit association test","Replication","Virtual reality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a registered replication study on body ownership perceptions and attitudes stage 000000000001 registered report protocol","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.26780176.v1","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776425","ids":["itu-97013898-f506-45c3-afa3-980898eb3eee"],"keyword":["Embodiment","implicit association test","replication","virtual reality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-468D2B80961121CA64B860DEF45AE540"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.26780176.v1","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://springernature.figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/A_registered_replication_study_on_body-ownership_perceptions_and_attitudes_Stage_1_Registered_Report_Protocol_/26780176","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Olga","key":"iaryginapau","last":"Iarygina","locid":"977ec015-b047-44f1-8aa9-bda7d606c977","name":"Iarygina, Olga","orcid":"0000-0002-2419-9638","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"hornbækpau","last":"Hornbæk","name":"Hornbæk, Kasper","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"410df04c-bc1d-4cd3-9726-c35469ddab8c","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"]},"sid":["itu-97013898-f506-45c3-afa3-980898eb3eee"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A registered replication study on body-ownership, perceptions, and attitudes [Stage 1 Registered Report Protocol]","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776425","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Youyou et al. (1) estimated the replicability of more than 14,000 psychology papers using a machine learning model, trained on main texts of 388 replicated studies. The authors identified mean replicability scores of psychological subfields. They also verified the causality of the model predictions; correlations between model predictions and study details the model was not trained on (i.e., P value and sample size) were reported. In attempting replication, we identified important shortcomings of the approach and findings. First, the training data contain duplicated paper entries. Second, our analysis shows that the model predictions also correlate with variables that are not causal to replicability (e.g., language style). These issues impede the validity of the model output and thereby paint an erroneous picture of replication rates of the psychological science. In this letter, we attempt to mitigate these issues and nuance the findings of the original paper.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Mottelson, Aske","Kontogiorgos, Dimosthenis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-695310"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Humboldt University of Berlin"],"keyword":["Causal inference","Machine learning","Model validation","Psychology","Replicability"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"replicating replicability modeling of psychology papers","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2309496120","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-695310","identifier":[{"id":"85166785059","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f5d9b8dd-0952-4d4b-8e55-33c22a03f559"],"keyword":["Causal inference","Machine learning","Model validation","Psychology","Replicability"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0397ACDB0FA61D27267F0D072FFEE314"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/108495569/mottelson-kontogiorgos-2023-replicating-replicability-modeling-of-psychology-papers.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2309496120","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://www.pnas.org/doi/epdf/10.1073/pnas.2309496120","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design, Digital Societies and Participation Section","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"humboldt university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Humboldt University of Berlin","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"410df04c-bc1d-4cd3-9726-c35469ddab8c","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Dimosthenis","key":"kontogiorgospau","last":"Kontogiorgos","name":"Kontogiorgos, Dimosthenis","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1073/pnas.2309496120","issn":["00278424","10916490"],"issn_org":["00278424"],"issue":"33","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","publisher":"The National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","volume":"120"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85166785059"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f5d9b8dd-0952-4d4b-8e55-33c22a03f559"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Replicating replicability modeling of psychology papers","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-695310","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Screenshots are ubiquitous in mobile computing, yet poorly understood. This paper advances our understanding of reasons for capturing, storing, and sharing screenshots. A crowdsourced user study was conducted where 52 participants shared personal screenshots from their phones, alongside textual reasons for why they were captured. Using mixed methods analyses we uncover common and uncommon screenshot practices that have not previously been documented. By analyzing the language used to describe reasons for taking screenshots, we document a variety of motivations for screenshot captures that provide opportunities for design. We furthermore report nine overarching themes in contemporary mobile screenshot use, considerably extending the currently held view of screenshots as a type of social computing. To inform design, we propose novel screenshot-centered interaction concepts that bridge the empirical findings. Last, we position screenshotting as a style of mobile interaction, which we argue is an undeveloped opportunity for advancing interactivity for mobile computing.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Mottelson, Aske"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-695270"],"event":{"acronym":"DIS","title":"ACM Conference on Designing Interactive Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Crowdsourcing","Data set","Empirical study","Human Computer Interaction","Mobile computing","Mobile interaction","Qualitative data","Quantitative data","Screenshots","User behavior","User studies"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"why do people take screenshots on their smartphones","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3563657.3596067","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-695270","ids":["itu-97270991-4f9e-43eb-856d-30b06c256643"],"keyword":["crowdsourcing","data set","empirical study","human computer interaction","mobile computing","mobile interaction","qualitative data","quantitative data","screenshots","user behavior","user studies"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-566ED2355A894AD30B095563FFF574E4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3563657.3596067","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3563657.3596067","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design, Digital Societies and Participation Section","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"410df04c-bc1d-4cd3-9726-c35469ddab8c","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3563657.3596067","isbn":["9781450398930"],"pages":"740-752","place":"New York, NY, USA","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","series":"DIS '23","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2023 ACM Designing Interactive Systems Conference"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-97270991-4f9e-43eb-856d-30b06c256643"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Why Do People Take Screenshots on Their Smartphones?","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-695270","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Body ownership illusions (BOIs) occur when participants experience that their actual body is replaced by a body shown in virtual reality (VR). Based on a systematic review of the cumulative evidence on BOIs from 111 research articles published in 2010 to 2021, this article summarizes the findings of empirical studies of BOIs. Following the PRISMA guidelines, the review points to diverse experimental practices for inducing and measuring body ownership. The two major components of embodiment measurement, body ownership and agency, are examined. The embodiment of virtual avatars generally leads to modest body ownership and slightly higher agency. We also find that BOI research lacks statistical power and standardization across tasks, measurement instruments, and analysis approaches. Furthermore, the reviewed studies showed a lack of clarity in fundamental terminology, constructs, and theoretical underpinnings. These issues restrict scientific advances on the major components of BOIs, and together impede scientific rigor and theory-building.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Mottelson, Aske","Muresan, Andreea","Hornbæk, Kasper","Makransky, Guido"],"cuid":["S02-095.42-716292"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Body ownership illusions","Embodiment","Meta analysis","Systematic review","Virtual reality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a systematic review and meta analysis of the effectiveness of body ownership illusions in virtual reality","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/374646425/A_Systematic_Review_and_Meta_analysis_of_the_Efectiveness_of_Body.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-095.42-716292","identifier":[{"id":"85163876485","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-3a152ca1-22d6-4306-92e2-f9d9c9c8786b","ku-3e95d025-1678-4d6f-963a-03cd08e40588"],"keyword":["Virtual reality","body ownership illusions","embodiment","meta-analysis","systematic review"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-AF49DE4767C9FB8943728FCA1DB51B37"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/374646425/A_Systematic_Review_and_Meta_analysis_of_the_Efectiveness_of_Body.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/98268520/3590767.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design, Digital Societies and Participation Section","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centred Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"410df04c-bc1d-4cd3-9726-c35469ddab8c","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Andreea","key":"muresanpau","last":"Muresan","locid":"a23fb51b-fd17-49d2-8da4-f1db78093497","name":"Muresan, Andreea","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Kasper","key":"hornbækpau","last":"Hornbæk","locid":"148d1e88-e306-46e1-9218-f420470b1ce1","name":"Hornbæk, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0001-8742-1198","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Guido","key":"makranskypau","last":"Makransky","locid":"945b6930-6c8f-49a5-b8ed-531dc0f8daef","name":"Makransky, Guido","orcid":"0000-0003-1862-7824","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3590767","issn":["10730516","15577325"],"issn_org":["10730516"],"journal":"ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction","pages":"1-43","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"ACM Transactions on Computer-Human Interaction","title_org":"A C M Transactions on Computer - Human Interaction"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85163876485"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3a152ca1-22d6-4306-92e2-f9d9c9c8786b","ku-3e95d025-1678-4d6f-963a-03cd08e40588"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of the Effectiveness of Body Ownership Illusions in Virtual Reality","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.42-716292","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Vaccine hesitancy poses one of the largest threats to global health. Informing people about the collective benefit of vaccination has great potential in increasing vaccination intentions. This research investigates the potential for engaging experiences in immersive virtual reality (VR) to strengthen participants’ understanding of community immunity, and therefore, their intention to get vaccinated. In a pre-registered lab-in-the-field intervention study, participants were recruited in a public park (tested: n= 232 , analyzed: n= 222). They were randomly assigned to experience the collective benefit of community immunity in a gamified immersive virtual reality environment (23 of sample), or to receive the same information via text and images (13 of sample). Before and after the intervention, participants indicated their intention to take up a hypothetical vaccine for a new COVID-19 strain (0–100 scale) and belief in vaccination as a collective responsibility (1–7 scale). The study employs a crossover design (participants later received a second treatment), but the primary outcome is the effect of the first treatment on vaccination intention. After the VR treatment, for participants with less-than-maximal vaccination intention, intention increases by 9.3 points (95% CI: 7.0 to 11.5,p<0.001). The text-and-image treatment raises vaccination intention by 3.3 points (difference in effects: 5.8, 95% CI: 2.0 to 9.5,p=0.003). The VR treatment also increases collective responsibility by 0.82 points (95% CI: 0.37 to 1.27,p<0.001). The results suggest that VR interventions are an effective tool for boosting vaccination intention, and that they can be applied “in the wild”—providing a complementary method for vaccine advocacy.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Vandeweerdt, Clara","Luong, Tiffany","Atchapero, Michael","Mottelson, Aske","Holz, Christian","Makransky, Guido","Böhm, Robert"],"cuid":["S02-099.60-038394"],"filters":{"added":"2022-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","ETH Zürich","Swiss Federal Institute of Technology Zürich","University of Vienna"],"keyword":["Covid 19","Human behaviour","Preventive medicine"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"virtual reality reduces covid 000000000019 vaccine hesitancy in the wild a randomized trial","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-08120-4","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.60-038394","identifier":[{"id":"35301359","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85126538824","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC8928717","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["itu-da7a4c8f-fe1d-4190-b257-cca90b2dec3f","ku-aaf9366c-e7a3-42c1-b2db-aa74abe5960c"],"keyword":["COVID-19","Human behaviour","Preventive medicine"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology","Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-FD5A95911F462B9888184344EBA29743"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-022-08120-4","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/319414541/s41598_022_08120_4.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science, Danish and Comparative Politics – Behavior, Institutions, and Methods","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science, Department of Political Science","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science, Research Group on Climate, Environment and Change (REGROUP)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ch","key":"eth zürich","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"ETH Zürich","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"ch","key":"swiss federal institute of technology zürich","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Swiss Federal Institute of Technology Zürich","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Clara","key":"vandeweerdtpau","last":"Vandeweerdt","locid":"c7f288d6-0ac0-4741-a571-2a858e03dce1","name":"Vandeweerdt, Clara","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Tiffany","key":"luongpau","last":"Luong","name":"Luong, Tiffany","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Michael","key":"atchaperopau","last":"Atchapero","name":"Atchapero, Michael","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 4","first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"3d052bd9-fd04-499a-bb39-67a2be5ef1e1","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Christian","key":"holzpau","last":"Holz","name":"Holz, Christian","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Guido","key":"makranskypau","last":"Makransky","locid":"945b6930-6c8f-49a5-b8ed-531dc0f8daef","name":"Makransky, Guido","orcid":"0000-0003-1862-7824","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Robert","key":"böhmpau","last":"Böhm","locid":"34398bf5-ecfe-4590-be77-4e9231b4e334","name":"Böhm, Robert","orcid":"0000-0001-6806-0374","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35301359"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-022-08120-4","issn":["20452322"],"issue":"1","journal":"Scientific Reports","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85126538824"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-da7a4c8f-fe1d-4190-b257-cca90b2dec3f","ku-aaf9366c-e7a3-42c1-b2db-aa74abe5960c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Virtual reality reduces COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy in the wild : a randomized trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.60-038394","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The relation between the body and computer interfaces has undergone several shifts since the advent of computing. In early models of interaction, the body was treated as a periphery to the mind, much like a keyboard is peripheral to a computer. The goal of the interface designers was to optimize the information flow between the brain and the computer, using these imperfect peripheral devices. Toward the end of the previous century the social body, as well as the material body and its physical manipulation skills started receiving increased consideration from interaction designers. The goal of the interface designer shifted, requiring the designer to understand the role of the body in a given context, and adapting the interface to respect the social context and to make use of the tacit knowledge that the body has of how the physical world functions. Currently, we are witnessing another shift in the role of the body. It is no longer merely something that requires consideration for interface design. Instead, advances in technology and our understanding of interaction allows the body to become part of the interface. We call these body-based user interfaces. We identify four ways in which the body becomes part of the interface: (1) The interaction might occur on or in the human body, for example using implanted tactile stimulation or touch interfaces on the body. Here the material of the body becomes part of the interface. (2) The interaction changes the morphology of the body and corresponding control structures, for example by providing users with additional skills, such as drawing or playing instruments or additional limbs that help complete complex tasks. Here the shape of the body, or the corresponding ability to act, is affected by the interface. (3) The interaction engages with or modifies how we perceive the world, for example by manipulating the sense of direction in VR or allowing users to experience non-existent stimuli, such as mid-air friction. Here, the idiosyncrasies of multi-modal perception and perceptive acts become part of the user interface. Finally, (4) the interaction might engage with the experience of having a body, for example by manipulating the sense of body ownership, location, or agency. Here the introspective access to one’s own body is used in the design of the interface. In this chapter, we present a brief history of the body’s role in human–computer interaction, leading up to a definition of body-based user interfaces. We follow this by presenting examples of interfaces that reflect the different ways in which interfaces can be body-based. We conclude by presenting outlooks on benefits, drawbacks, and possible futures of body-based user interfaces.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Strohmeier, Paul","Mottelson, Aske","Pohl, Henning","McIntosh, Jess","Knibbe, Jarrod","Bergström, Joanna","Jansen, Yvonne","Hornbæk, Kasper"],"cuid":["S03-096.22-000253"],"filters":{"added":"2022-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","France","Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CNRS - Centre national de la recherche scientifique","Inria","Saarland University","University of Melbourne"],"keyword":["Body Based User Interfaces","Human Computer Interaction","Morphological Augmentation","Perceptual Modulation","Tactile Stimulation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"body based user interfaces","src":["aau","itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/chapters/edit/10.4324/9780429321542-39/body-based-user-interfaces-paul-strohmeier-aske-mottelson-henning-pohl-jess-mcintosh-jarrod-knibbe-joanna-bergstr%C3%B6m-yvonne-jansen-kasper-hornb%C3%A6k","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-096.22-000253","identifier":[{"id":"85141187884","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-04e03469-4fcc-4d4a-b5a3-75a1c7edfa05","itu-b5e71284-6400-46d1-98e7-837b85b0e9bd","ku-6b650faf-cadf-4093-aee4-de7399ed971c"],"keyword":["Body-Based User Interfaces","Human-Computer Interaction","Morphological Augmentation","Perceptual Modulation","Tactile Stimulation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-D0C333181F53D2799C0BACAE85805D9A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/chapters/edit/10.4324/9780429321542-39/body-based-user-interfaces-paul-strohmeier-aske-mottelson-henning-pohl-jess-mcintosh-jarrod-knibbe-joanna-bergstr%C3%B6m-yvonne-jansen-kasper-hornb%C3%A6k","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"saarland university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Saarland University","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design, Digital Societies and Participation Section","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"D